      "text": "To determine the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\), we need to analyze the function by considering the values of \\( x \\) that lead to different possible cases within the absolute value functions. These cases are determined by the critical points where the expressions inside the absolute values change their sign, i.e., specifically when \\( x = -1 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nLet’s break it down into cases based on these critical points:\n\n**Case 1:** For \\( x < -1 \\)\n\nIn this case, both expressions inside the absolute values will be negative:\n\\[ |x+1| = -(x+1) = -x - 1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x + 1 \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ G(x) = (-x - 1) - (-x + 1) = -x - 1 + x - 1 = -2 \\]\n\nSince \\( x < -1 \\), this evaluates the same expression multiple times as it alternates within each interval.\n\n**Case 2:** For \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\)\n\nHere, the expression inside \\( |x-1| \\) is negative, while the expression inside \\( |x+1| \\) is positive or zero.\n\\[ |x+1| = (x+1) \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x + 1 \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ G(x) = (x + 1) - (-x + 1) = x + 1 + x - 1 = 2x \\]\n\nFor \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), implying that the ranges vary from \\( -2 \\) to \\( 2 \\).\n\n**Case 3:** For \\( x \\geq 1 \\)\n\nIn this case, both expression inside absolute values will be positive or zero:\n\\[ |x+1| = (x+1) \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = (x-1) \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ G(x) = (x + 1) - (x - 1) = x + 1 - x + 1 = 2 \\]\n\nFor \\( x \\geq 1 \\), this always evaluates the same expression multiple times based on each point where it changes within the interval.\n\nCombining all results from above, we have the following intervals:\n\n- \\( (-\\infty, -1): \\quad G(x) = -2 \\)\n- \\( [-1, 1): \\quad G(x) = 2x \\rightarrow [2, 4] \\)\n- \\( [1, \\infty): \\quad G(x) = 2 \\)\n\nTherefore, the range of \\( G(x) \\) covers from \\(-2\\) to \\(4\\). Hence, the range of \\(G(x)\\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{[-2, 4]} \\]",
