      "text": "The given function is \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\). To find the range of this function, we need to determine the possible values that \\( G(x) \\) can take for all \\( x \\). We do this by analyzing the behavior of the function at different points based on the definition of the absolute value function.\n\n1. **Identify the critical points of the absolute value function:**\n   The critical points are where the arguments of the absolute value functions change sign. These points are \\( x = -1 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\).\n\n2. **Separate the function into different intervals:**\n   We will consider the function \\( G(x) \\) in the intervals \\( (-\\infty, -1) \\), \\( [-1, 1] \\), and \\( (1, \\infty) \\).\n\n   - **Interval \\( (-\\infty, -1) \\):**\n     Here, \\( x + 1 < 0 \\) and \\( x - 1 < 0 \\). So, \\( |x+1| = -x-1 \\) and \\( |x-1| = -x+1 \\). Therefore,\n     \\[\n     G(x) = (-x-1) - (-x+1) = -x-1 + x - 1 = -2.\n     \\]\n     So, \\( G(x) = -2 \\) for all \\( x \\in (-\\infty, -1) \\).\n\n   - **Interval \\( [-1, 1] \\):**\n     Here, \\( x + 1 > 0 \\) and \\( x - 1 \\leq 0 \\). So, \\( |x+1| = x+1 \\) and \\( |x-1| = -x+1 \\). Therefore,\n     \\[\n     G(x) = (x+1) - (-x+1) = x+1 + x - 1 = 2x.\n     \\]\n     At \\( x = -1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2(-1) = -2 \\). At \\( x = 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2(1) = 2 \\). So, \\( G(x) \\) ranges from \\(-2\\) to \\(2\\) for \\( x \\in [-1, 1] \\).\n\n   - **Interval \\( (1, \\infty) \\):**\n     Here, \\( x + 1 > 0 \\) and \\( x - 1 > 0 \\). So, \\( |x+1| = x+1 \\) and \\( |x-1| = x-1 \\). Therefore,\n     \\[\n     G(x) = (x+1) - (x-1) = x+1 - x + 1 = 2.\n     \\]\n     So, \\( G(x) = 2 \\) for all \\( x \\in (1, \\infty) \\).\n\n3. **Combine the results from all intervals:**\n   From the analysis, we see that at \\( x = -1 \\), \\( x = 1 \\), and \\( G(x) = 2 \\) for all other \\( x \\), the function \\( G(x) \\) takes all values in the interval \\([-2, 2]\\). Therefore, the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\) is \\(\\boxed{[-2, 2]}\\).",
