      "text": "We have:\n\\begin{align*}\nG(x) &= |x+1|-|x-1| \\\\\n&= \\begin{cases}\n(1+x)-(x-1) = 2 \\hspace{15.15cm} & x \\geq 0 \\\\\n(x+1)-(1-x) = 2x \\hspace{15.15cm} & -1 \\leq x < 0 \\\\\n(-x-1)-(x-1) = -2x \\hspace{15.15cm} & -1 < x \\end{cases}\\\\\n&= \\begin{cases}\n2 \\hspace{5.7cm} & x \\geq 0 \\\\\n2x \\hspace{11.9cm} & -1 \\leq x < 0 \\\\\n-2x \\hspace{12.5cm} & -1 < x \\end{cases}\n\\end{align*}\nSince $2 \\geq 2x > -2x>0$ for $-1<x<1$, we deduce that the range is $\\boxed{\\left[0,2\\right]}$.\n\nAdd: \"where $x \\neq 0$ because when $x = 0$, $|x+1| = |x-1|$ so $G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| = 0$.\"\nTo solve and find the range of the function\n\n\\[\nG(x) = |x+1| - |x-1|\n\\]\n\nwe need to examine the behavior of \\( G(x) \\) over different intervals of \\( x \\). \n\n1. **Case 1: \\( x \\geq 1 \\)**\n\n   For \\( x \\geq 1 \\), both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are non-negative, so we can drop the absolute values:\n\n   \\[\n   G(x) = (x+1) - (x-1) = x+1 - x + 1 = 2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Case 2: \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\)**\n\n   For \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), \\( x+1 \\) is non-negative and \\( x-1 \\) is negative:\n\n   \\[\n   G(x) = (x+1) - (-(x-1)) = x+1 + x-1 = 2x\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), the expression \\( 2x \\) ranges from \\( 2(-1) = -2 \\) to \\( 2(1) = 2 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   -2 \\leq 2x < 2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Case 3: \\( x < -1 \\)**\n\n   For \\( x < -1 \\), both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are negative, so we drop the absolute values:\n\n   \\[\n   G(x) = (-(x+1)) - (-(x-1)) = -x-1 + x-1 = -2\n   \\]\n\nCombining these results, we observe that:\n\n- For \\( x \\geq 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2 \\)\n- For \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2x \\) which ranges from \\( -2 \\) to \\( 2 \\)\n- For \\( x < -1 \\), \\( G(x) = -2 \\)\n\nThe expression of \\( G(x) \\) can be written as a piecewise-continuous formulation:\n\n\\[\nG(x) = \\begin{cases}\n2 & x \\geq 1 \\\\\n-2x & -1 \\leq x < 1 \\\\\n2x & x < -1\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe key observation is that as \\( x \\) varies across its domain, \\( G(x) \\) fluctuates continuously between \\( 2 \\) and \\( -2 \\) within individual intervals. Outside these intervals, there are specific values that are not covered.\n\nHence, the range of \\( G(x) \\) is entirely covered by these ranges. Therefore, among all points on a number line, including the values at the boundaries and within each interval, there are no gaps.\n\nThus, the total range is given by the closed interval:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{[0, 2]}\n\\]",
